        <body>
            <div xml:id="d339">

                <pb facs="0570" n="470" sameAs="frus:frus1884/pg470"/>

                <head>No. 339.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Foster to Mr.
                    Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-12-29"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 29, 1883</date>.
                        (Received January 19, 1884.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 132.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: As supplementary to my No. 121, of the 15th
                    instant, in regard to slavery in Cuba, I desire to add, as an indication of the
                    progress being made towards complete emancipation, that it appears from the
                    official report just published that on the 6th ultimo there were in Cuba 99,566
                        “<hi rendition="#italic">patrocinados”</hi> (the legal term applied to those
                    still held in bondage). In the year 1868, before the Moret emancipation law was
                    enacted, there were registered 385,356 slaves.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN W. FOSTER.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
